const title = "Rooms & Exits Chapter 3 Level 13 Walkthrough: Kids Bedroom 1";
const description = "Rooms & Exits Chapter 3 Level 13 walkthrough for Kids Bedroom 1: the wooden box whose green panels read 74 and 4, the desk calculator that turns 48 x 5 into 240 and then 240 x 74 into 17,760, the 'Fill the white squares' tablet, the butterfly pattern rule, the six animal knobs, the four-colour switch chest, and the last two minutes — the static matchstick lid, the animal clue card, the cabinet drawer that opens at 8:21 and the exit arrow at 8:43. Every printed number was read twice from separate frames.";

const faq = [
  {
    q: "What is the code in Rooms & Exits Chapter 3 Level 13 Kids Bedroom 1?",
    a: "There is no single typed code that opens the room. The two numbers the level actually puts in front of you are printed on the wooden box: <b>74</b> on its front green panel and <b>4</b> on its right-hand green panel. The desk calculator is the level's arithmetic station, and the run we worked from uses it in two steps: <b>48 x 5 = 240</b>, then <b>240 x 74 = 17,760</b>. The <b>17,760</b> reading was taken twice from the calculator's LCD at two separate seconds, and 240 x 74 does equal 17,760.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I get 17,760 on the calculator?",
    a: "The calculator on the desk is a real working calculator, not a static prop. In the recording the display shows <b>48*5</b> at 6:45, <b>240*</b> at 6:46, and <b>17760</b> at 6:48 and again at 6:50. That is a two-step calculation, not a puzzle chain: 48 x 5 = 240, then 240 x 74 = 17,760, with the 74 coming off the wooden box's green panel. The result sits on the LCD as a plain five-digit number, no decimal point.",
  },
  {
    q: "Where is the wooden box with 74 on it?",
    a: "It is the same box twice: at 1:24 the game pulls it up as a full inspect view, and at 5:24 you can see it sitting on the top shelf of the wardrobe, next to the folded clothes and above the green backpack. It is light wood with bright green side panels, an X-shaped wooden brace on each panel, and the numbers <b>74</b> (front) and <b>4</b> (right side) printed in pale green. Its lid holds a dark matchstick segment display that is completely unlit the first time you see it.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the matchstick puzzle?",
    a: "At 7:00 the box's lid becomes a full-screen close-up: a dark panel in a wooden frame, with digits laid out in matchsticks, burnt heads and all, and one match in the last position sitting <b>diagonally</b> instead of in a groove. That diagonal is the only obviously 'wrong' stick on the board. The board is <b>static</b> — we pulled every one-second frame from 7:00 to 7:26 and they are pixel-identical, so nothing moves and nothing is typed during those 27 seconds. Our crops agree on the first three positions reading <b>1, 2, 3</b>; the fourth position reads as 4, 8 or 9 depending on the crop, so we do not print it as a code. Nothing is entered anywhere after the board either — the calculator's 17,760 is never transferred onto it on camera.",
  },
  {
    q: "What does the tablet ask for?",
    a: "The tablet lying on the rug opens a grid puzzle with the instruction <b>FILL THE WHITE SQUARES</b>. The board is a purple patterned grid scattered with white cells, and you place orange stud-topped blocks into the white cells. The recording clears it in about forty seconds, between 0:06 and 0:42. No digits or letters appear on the tablet, so it is a spatial puzzle rather than a code lock.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the butterfly puzzle rule?",
    a: "A framed picture of a tree full of butterflies at 4:18 carries the level's most explicit instruction. It reads: <b>DON'T COUNT HOW MANY BUTTERFLIES THERE ARE! COUNT THE NUMBER OF BUTTERFLY PATTERNS THAT HAVE THE SAME COLOR</b>. So the answer is a count of matching wing patterns, not a count of butterflies. The picture on the easel on the left of the room is the same butterfly tree, which is how the level tells you the two are connected.",
  },
  {
    q: "What are the six animal knobs?",
    a: "The dresser under the desk has three drawers and six round knobs, each printed with an animal or object: a <b>green frog</b> and a <b>green crocodile</b> on the top drawer, a <b>white hippo</b> and a <b>yellow lion</b> on the middle drawer, a <b>yellow duck</b> and a <b>yellow mushroom</b> on the bottom drawer. The top pair is joined by a dark cord with a knot in the middle. The run works the dresser from about 7:56 to 8:20 but <b>never shows it opening</b> — the shot cuts away with all three drawers still shut, so this page prints no knob order.",
  },
  {
    q: "Where do I find the key and the matches?",
    a: "The <b>key</b> is already in the keyhole of the top desk drawer at 5:00 — the close-up shows a small silver key turned in a white drawer front with a gold handle. Turning it opens the drawer. The <b>Matches</b> are a separate pickup at 5:18, shown as a brown matchbox with two rows of gold-headed matches and labelled <b>Matches</b> in the inventory. The matches are what the box-lid matchstick puzzle runs on.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the chest with the coloured switches?",
    a: "At 6:00 the game opens a wooden chest with a brass latch. Below the latch sits a panel divided into <b>four coloured modules — red, yellow, green and purple — each holding four brass toggle switches</b>, sixteen switches in total, all off. It is a state puzzle, not a keypad: you are setting a pattern of on/off switches rather than typing digits.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the sliding-block puzzle in this room?",
    a: "A full-screen sliding-block board with coloured square blocks, each carrying a smiley face, runs from about 2:06 to 3:36. It is the longest single puzzle in the level — a minute and a half of real time in the recording, with the room clock ticking. We are not publishing a move list for it: the clip is cut and the block states change between sampled frames, so a guessed sequence would waste more of your time than solving it.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is Kids Bedroom 1 room 13 of Chapter 3?",
    a: "Yes. Chapter 3 is <i>Murder Dressed in White</i> and its fifteen rooms run: Dining Room, Living Room, Study Room, Guest Toilet, Workshop, Guest Bedroom, Master Bathroom, Kids Playroom, Backyard, Garden, Kitchen, Driveway, <b>Kids Bedroom</b>, Storage Room, Garage. Kids Bedroom is room <b>13 of 15</b>, straight after the Driveway and before the Storage Room.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does the recording show the level being completed?",
    a: "No, and this time we can say that from the whole file rather than a truncated one. The run is <b>8:43</b> long and plays through to the last frame. It ends on the room with an orange <b>exit arrow</b> floating over the closed door, then fades to black at 8:43. There is no results card, no star rating and no reward payout on camera: the coin counter in the top-left reads <b>3,340</b> at 0:05 and still <b>3,340</b> at 8:42, so this page prints no reward figure for Kids Bedroom 1.",
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a.replace(/<[^>]+>/g, "") },
  })),
};
