    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 11 · 6:45 · the calculator</span>
      <strong>48 × 5 = 240, then 240 × 74 = 17,760</strong>
      The calculator on the desk is not scenery — it is the level's arithmetic station, and the run uses it in two steps. At 6:45 the LCD reads <b>48*5</b>; at 6:46 it reads <b>240*</b>; at 6:48 and again at 6:50 it reads <b>17760</b>. So the chain is 48 × 5 = 240, then 240 × 74 = 17,760, with the 74 coming straight off the wooden box's front panel. The result sits on the LCD as five plain digits with no decimal point.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l13-calculator-17760.jpg" alt="The desk calculator in Kids Bedroom 1 with its LCD reading 17760" width="1200" height="676" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=405s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6:45</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=408s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6:48</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 12 · 7:00 to 7:26 · the lid</span>
      <strong>The matchstick board that does not move</strong>
      The box lid comes back as a full-screen close-up at 7:00: a dark panel in a wooden frame, the digits laid out in individual matchsticks with burnt heads, and one match in the last position lying <b>diagonally</b> across the grooves instead of inside them. Here is the thing worth knowing before you spend time on it: the board is <b>static</b>. We compared every one-second frame from 7:00 to 7:25 pixel by pixel — the mean difference never passes <b>6/255</b>, which is a slow camera push-in, not a puzzle being worked. No match moves, no digit changes, nothing is entered; at 7:26 the shot simply cuts back to the room. At one frame per second our crops read the first three positions as <b>1, 2, 3</b>; the fourth reads as 4, 8 or 9 depending on the crop, so this page does not print a code for it. The lid is never opened again in the run, which also means the <b>17,760</b> is never transferred onto it on camera.
