      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=447s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">7:27</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=461s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">7:41</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=463s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">7:43</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 14 · 7:45 to 7:56 · the clue card</span>
      <strong>Six animals, two columns</strong>
      A <b>Clue</b> card arrives in the inventory: six animal drawings in two columns of three, and it is the reference sheet for the six knobs on the dresser. The top row reads <b>lion</b> and <b>crocodile</b> in every crop we took; the bottom row is a <b>frog</b> and a grey horned animal that comes back as a hippo in some crops and a rhino in others; the middle pair changed between fox, monkey, bear and chick. Read the card as six creatures, then find those six creatures printed on the knobs.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l13-animal-clue-card.jpg" alt="The clue card in Kids Bedroom 1 showing six animal drawings in two columns" width="1200" height="676" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=465s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">7:45</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=471s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">7:51</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 15 · 7:56 to 8:31 · dresser, then cabinet</span>
      <strong>The knobs, and the drawer that actually opens</strong>
      From about 7:56 the animal-knob dresser is on screen and the six knobs are being worked against the clue card. Here is the wrinkle: <b>the run never shows you that dresser opening</b>. At 8:20 the camera is still on the six animal knobs, all three drawers shut, and then the shot cuts away — there is no pop, no drawer, no reveal.
      <br><br>
      What opens at <b>8:21</b> is a different piece of furniture: a light-wood unit with books, a blue hexagonal box and a small rabbit figurine on top, and one wide drawer pulled out and packed with neatly folded clothes — red-and-white stripes, green, lime, magenta. The game hands you two <b>puzzle pieces</b> out of it: a bright green triangle at 8:22 and a small pale-blue piece at 8:26. If you are hunting for the last tangram pieces, this cabinet is where they are, not the dresser.
