    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p><b>You keep looking for a keypad.</b> There isn't one, and that is deliberate. Kids Bedroom 1 does its locking with objects — a box lid, a switch panel, a dresser of knobs — rather than with a number pad on a wall. The only digits the room actually asks you to produce come out of the calculator.</p>
    <p><b>You read 74 and 4 as a single code.</b> They are not a two-digit and a one-digit code sitting next to each other; they are two numbers on two different faces of the same box, and the level expects you to do something with them. The run we worked from multiplies: 48 × 5 first, then 240 × 74, ending on 17,760.</p>
    <p><b>The box lid is dark and looks broken.</b> It is supposed to be. The matchstick display is unlit when you first inspect the box at 1:24 and only fills in once you have the <b>Matches</b> from 5:18. If you found the box early and thought it was decoration, go back to it after the drawer.</p>
    <p><b>You keep waiting for the matchsticks to move.</b> They don't. From 7:00 to 7:26 not a single stick changes, so there is no pattern to "solve" by watching it — it is a still arrangement, and the diagonal match in the last position is the only stick out of its groove. Do not sit on it hoping for a change: the room's real endgame is the dresser and the tangram, and the lid is never touched again after 7:26.</p>
    <p><b>The butterfly picture feels like a trick question.</b> It is, but the wording is precise: <b>count the number of butterfly patterns that have the same color</b>. Counting the insects gets you a number that is too big and always wrong. Group by colour scheme first, then count the group.</p>
    <p><b>The sliding-block board eats your time.</b> Plan for it. It is the one puzzle in this room that the recording spends more than a minute on, and there is no hint that solves it for you. Leave it until the quick pickups are done so you are not losing progress elsewhere while you grind it.</p>

    <h2>Where Kids Bedroom 1 sits in Chapter 3<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Chapter 3 is <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and its fifteen rooms run from the Dining Room through the house and out into the garden: Dining Room, Living Room, Study Room, Guest Toilet, Workshop, Guest Bedroom, Master Bathroom, Kids Playroom, Backyard, Garden, Kitchen, Driveway, <b>Kids Bedroom</b>, Storage Room, Garage. Kids Bedroom is room <b>13 of 15</b> — the third-from-last room in the chapter, straight after the Driveway and before the Storage Room.</p>
    <p>Mechanically it is a reset after Kitchen. Kitchen was a chain of dependencies in one big room; this is a small room where most puzzles stand alone, and the only chain is the one that ends at the box: numbers on the box, calculator on the desk, matches from the drawer, display in the lid. That makes it a good room to clear in one pass — grab the pickups first, then settle in for the sliding blocks.</p>
    <p>The tail of the run confirms the shape of it: there is no final boss lock. After the lid at 7:00 the player just clears what is left around the room — the clue card, the dresser, the tangram — and the exit arrow appears at 8:42 with nothing left to type.</p>

