    <h2>What this page could not verify<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <div class="warn">
      Five things, stated plainly so you know which parts of this page are reads and which are reconstruction. This pass used the complete 8:43 file, so items the truncated copy forced onto this list are gone.
      <br><br>
      <b>1. The digit string on the matchstick lid.</b> Still not printable, but for a different reason than before. The board is <b>static</b>: across the 26 one-second frames from 7:00 to 7:25 the mean pixel difference never exceeds <b>6/255</b> — slow camera drift, no stick moving — so the "cells change from second to second" line in the first version of this page was wrong. What we cannot settle is the reading. Separate crops of that one still agree on the first three positions as <b>1, 2, 3</b> and then split on the fourth — 4, 8 and 9 all came back at least once, and the cell count itself flips between four and five depending on where the crop edge falls. A pixel census of the panel finds 9 horizontal bars, 10 vertical sticks and the one diagonal, which is fewer sticks than a clean five-digit reading would need — some sticks are simply too dark against the panel to count. So the page prints no code for the lid. The calculator's <b>17760</b> is solid; it is just never entered onto this board on camera.
      <br><br>
      <b>2. The ending and the reward.</b> The file is complete now — 522 seconds, decoded to the last frame — and it still does not show a completion screen. The run ends at <b>8:43</b> on the room with the orange exit arrow over a closed door, then fades. No results card, no star rating, no payout animation, and the coin counter holds at <b>3,340</b> from 0:05 to 8:42. So the absence of a reward figure is now a fact about the video, not about a broken download.
      <br><br>
      <b>3. The move order for the knob, track and tangram puzzles.</b> The run never lets us see the animal-knob dresser open — the shot cuts from three shut drawers at 8:20 straight to a different cabinet at 8:21 — so there is no solved state to read a knob order off. The tangram does get finished on camera (key visible among the pieces at 8:37, taken at 8:40), but the run is sampled at one frame per second, so the placement order is not recoverable either. Those sections stay descriptive rather than prescriptive.
      <br><br>
      <b>4. Four of the six animals on the clue card.</b> The card is a two-column grid of six drawings. Lion and crocodile read the same in every crop. The bottom-right has come back as both a <b>hippo</b> and a <b>rhino</b>, and the middle pair as fox, monkey, bear and chick across different passes. We print the two we trust and leave the rest of the card unlabelled rather than pair the wrong creature to a knob.
      <br><br>
      <b>5. The significance of the 4 on the box's side panel.</b> The <b>74</b> is used: 240 × 74 = 17,760 is what the calculator lands on. The <b>4</b> on the right-hand panel is read just as reliably, but nothing in the full 8:43 run ever uses it — not the calculator, not the lid, not the exit. It may belong to a step outside this room. We are not going to invent a sum for it.
    </div>
